The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions concerning the Sudan, in particular resolutions 1591 (2005), 1651 (2005), 1665 (2006), 1672 (2006), 1713 (2006), 1779 (2007), 1841 (2008), 1891 (2009), 1945 (2010), 1982 (2011), 2035 (2012), 2138 (2014), 2200 (2015), 2265 (2016), 2340 (2017), 2400 (2018), 2455 (2019), 2508 (2020), 2562 (2021), 2620 (2022), 2676 (2023), 2724 (2024), 2725 (2024), 2736 (2024), and 2750 (2024), and the relevant statements of its President,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, independence, and territorial integrity of the Sudan,

Expressing grave concern at the continued escalation of violence, the devastating humanitarian catastrophe, and the widespread destruction across the Sudan, in particular in Darfur, Gezira, Khartoum, and the Kordofans, and deeply alarmed by the severe food insecurity, acute malnutrition, and famine conditions affecting millions of civilians,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks against civilians, civilian infrastructure, and humanitarian personnel, as well as ethnically motivated violence, widespread conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, recruitment and use of children, and attacks on internally displaced persons camps, including in Zamzam and Abu Shouk,

Calling upon all parties to the conflict to immediately cease hostilities, de-escalate the fighting, comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and honor their commitments under the Jeddah Declaration of Commitment to Protect the Civilians of Sudan,

Demanding that all parties allow and facilitate full, rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access across all borders and conflict lines to ensure the life-saving delivery of humanitarian assistance to all people in need throughout the Sudan,

Emphasizing the vital role played by the Panel of Experts on the Sudan in providing critical information and independent reporting on the implementation of the sanctions regime, arms embargo violations, illicit financial flows, and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights,

Taking note of the final report of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005),

Determining that the situation in the Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend until 12 March 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts originally appointed pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) and previously extended by resolutions 1779 (2007), 1841 (2008), 1945 (2010), 2035 (2012), 2138 (2014), 2200 (2015), 2265 (2016), 2340 (2017), 2400 (2018), 2455 (2019), 2508 (2020), 2562 (2021), 2620 (2022), 2676 (2023), and 2725 (2024), and expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding further extension no later than 12 February 2026;

2. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) concerning the Sudan an interim report on its activities no later than 12 August 2025, and to submit to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, a final report by 13 January 2026 with its findings and recommendations;

3. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide regular updates to the Committee, including on the illicit flow of weapons and military materiel into Darfur in violation of the arms embargo, the sources and networks facilitating these transfers, obstacles to humanitarian operations, and human rights violations and abuses;

4. Urges all parties and all Member States, as well as international, regional, and subregional organizations, to ensure full cooperation with the Panel of Experts and the safety and unhindered movement of its members throughout the Sudan;

5. Calls upon the Sudanese authorities to facilitate the timely issuance of visas and travel permits without unjustified delays to the Panel of Experts to enable the effective execution of its mandate;

6. Reminds all Member States of their obligations to strictly implement the targeted sanctions and the arms embargo measures reaffirmed in resolution 2750 (2024), and calls upon all Member States to refrain from external interference that fuels the conflict and instability, and to support efforts toward an immediate cessation of hostilities and a sustainable political resolution;

7. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

