Determining that the situation in the Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend until 12 March 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts originally appointed pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) and previously extended by resolutions 1779 (2007), 1841 (2008), 1945 (2010), 2035 (2012), 2138 (2014), 2200 (2015), 2265 (2016), 2340 (2017), 2400 (2018), 2455 (2019), 2508 (2020), 2562 (2021), 2620 (2022), 2676 (2023), and 2725 (2024), and expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding further extension no later than 12 February 2026;

2. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) concerning the Sudan an interim report on its activities no later than 12 August 2025, and to submit to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, a final report by 13 January 2026 with its findings and recommendations;

3. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide regular updates to the Committee, including on the illicit flow of weapons and military materiel into Darfur in violation of the arms embargo, the sources and networks facilitating these transfers, obstacles to humanitarian operations, and human rights violations and abuses, including conflict-related sexual violence and violations and abuses against children;

4. Urges all parties and all Member States, as well as international, regional, and subregional organizations, to ensure full cooperation with the Panel of Experts and the safety and unhindered movement of its members throughout the Sudan;

5. Encourages continued constructive cooperation and dialogue between the Sudanese authorities and the Panel of Experts, and calls upon the Sudanese authorities to facilitate the timely issuance of visas and travel permits to members of the Panel of Experts to enable the effective execution of its mandate;

6. Reminds all Member States of their obligations to strictly implement the targeted sanctions and the arms embargo measures reaffirmed in resolution 2750 (2024), calls upon all Member States to refrain from external interference that fuels the conflict and instability, and urges support for coordinated regional and international efforts, in particular those of the African Union, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD), and the Personal Envoy of the Secretary-General, toward an immediate cessation of hostilities and a sustainable political resolution;

